WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.200
Hello, and welcome to this video on the original Animation Blueprint.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:12.580
In this, we're just going to have a look at the Animation Blueprint that we replaced at the beginning.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:20.579
So, I've loaded up the Animation Blueprint, and first of all, we'll have a quick look at the Animation Graph.

4
00:00:20.579 --> 00:00:23.579
In here, there are a few noteworthy differences.

5
00:00:23.579 --> 00:00:32.619
For example, we're not splitting the animation by the spine, so that we can play and montage on different areas.

6
00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.619
We've just got one default slot, which will allow us to play a montage.

7
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:42.619
That would take up the entire body at any one time, regardless of if you're moving or not.

8
00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:48.119
The other section here, is we have a Control Rig.

9
00:00:48.119 --> 00:00:54.500
Now, as you can see here on the Control Rig, we've got the Should Do IK trace.

10
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.920
Now, the first thing is, it's only going to do this when it isn't falling.

11
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:09.079
And what this is here for, is this is for making sure the feet automatically touch the ground.

12
00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:14.079
When I was to come into the right hand side, you can see that we actually have a Control Rig class here,

13
00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:17.079
which I could find and open up.

14
00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:24.409
Now, this looks quite complicated, but what it's doing in most cases,

15
00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:27.409
is it's doing a line trace off the bottom of the feet.

16
00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.500
And it's seeing if it hits the ground.

17
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:39.500
And if it does, it's going to adjust the foot IK, so that the feet actually end up being perfectly on the ground.

18
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.500
And you can see this.

19
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:47.500
If I was to go back and change my default pawn back to the previous, the original one,

20
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.879
and push play, you can see this here.

21
00:01:52.879 --> 00:02:01.750
So at any one time, the feet are exactly on the floor.

22
00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:09.680
If I was to convert back to our original player character, and play that one,

23
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.870
you can already see at the moment that the feet are floating slightly.

24
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:17.870
That becomes even more pronounced when we go on an uneven level.

25
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:22.090
So the other thing that's different about this chart is,

26
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:29.090
you can see here that the locomotion state is being saved out into a cached pose.

27
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:36.090
However, it's not directly being used as an input for the default slot.

28
00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.439
If we double click on the main state,

29
00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:46.759
you can see that we actually have a relatively different state machine here.

30
00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:53.759
If we were to jump into locomotion, you can see this is where the cached pose is being used.

31
00:02:53.759 --> 00:03:00.580
Now the setup here looks quite a bit different to the previous one that we were working on.

32
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:09.620
And that's because we're using these elements, the To Falling and the To Land, state aliases.

33
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.580
And these are mainly used to clean up a graph.

34
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.580
Because standardly, if you were to build this setup,

35
00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.580
we would need to have a lot of links going through all of these different places

36
00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.580
to make sure that you could transition at the correct time.

37
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:33.580
To get to the To Falling node, we would need to either come from the locomotion or the land.

38
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.580
So we would need to have connections to connect up the jump

39
00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.580
or the fall loop from both the locomotion and both of the land.

40
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.770
One for each of these settings.

41
00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.770
Now, what this means is, for this falling, these two conduits,

42
00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:54.770
the Is Falling velocity Z and the Is Falling,

43
00:03:54.770 --> 00:04:02.770
are both accessible from the locomotion or the land states, because they're ticked on here.

44
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.120
And the same for the To Land.

45
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:13.180
This conduit, Is Not Falling, would be accessible from the jump or the fall loop.

46
00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.180
So these clear up a lot of the messy spaghetti that you would see.

47
00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:26.209
The other thing that's worth noting here is where we standardly went into the jump node,

48
00:04:26.209 --> 00:04:29.240
as soon as you weren't falling.

49
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.240
In this we have two conditions.

50
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:37.240
We're actually checking to see if the velocity is going up on the Z.

51
00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:44.240
So it's only going to go to the jump if your velocity heading upwards is quite strong.

52
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.240
Otherwise, it's going to jump immediately into the fall.

53
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.339
This means, and I'll demonstrate in a second,

54
00:04:50.339 --> 00:04:55.339
we're going to have smoother transitions if you just run off the edge.

55
00:04:55.339 --> 00:04:57.459
So if we check that out now.

56
00:04:57.459 --> 00:05:01.689
So this was the animation blueprint we put together.

57
00:05:01.689 --> 00:05:06.850
And as you can see, when she falls off, it's a rather odd animation.

58
00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:09.939
A combination of the jump.

59
00:05:09.939 --> 00:05:14.379
And here is the original blueprint.

60
00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:21.329
And as you can see, it's a lot smoother falling off.

61
00:05:21.329 --> 00:05:25.029
It's just going straight into the drop without the jump.

62
00:05:25.029 --> 00:05:29.259
So with that, that will bring us to the end of this video.

63
00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:34.259
So I mainly wanted to make sure that you could understand the difference between the

64
00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:40.259
animation blueprint we created and the one standardly you get with this template.

65
00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:42.959
Thank you for watching.
